\details{
\code{configure()} builds a configuration grid — the Cartesian product of all
specified modeling axes. Each row represents a distinct pipeline to
evaluate. This is the bridge between data preparation (\code{add_response()})
and model evaluation (\code{evaluate()}).

\strong{Outcome promotion:}

\code{add_response()} assigns \code{role = "response"} to joined variables.
\code{configure()} promotes exactly one response to \code{role = "outcome"} for
modeling. If only one response exists, it's auto-selected. Multiple
responses require explicit \code{outcome} specification.

\strong{Covariate expansion:}

When covariates are present, \code{expand_covariates} controls whether to
benchmark across different covariate subsets (power-set expansion) or
use all covariates in every configuration.

\strong{Reconfiguration:}

Can be called multiple times on the same object. Previous configuration
is overwritten, any prior outcome role is reverted to "response", and
everything a previous \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} or \code{ensemble()} earned is
dropped: the \code{evaluation}, \code{models} and \code{ensemble} slots return to their
empty state, and the \code{horizons_eval}, \code{horizons_fit} or
\code{horizons_ensemble} class goes with them. All of it is keyed to the
outcome that is being replaced. The validation verdict is cleared too,
but the record of outliers \code{validate()} already removed is kept, since
those rows stay removed, and so is a \code{select_training()} record, which
describes the rows rather than the outcome. Because neither was chosen for
the new outcome, \code{configure()} warns when rows were removed as response
outliers of a different outcome, and when a \code{select_training()} record
(other than \code{scope = "global"}) was drawn for properties that do not
include it.
This enables the \code{purrr::map()} multi-outcome pattern:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{c("SOC", "POM_C", "pH") |>
  purrr::map(~base |> configure(outcome = .x) |> evaluate() |> fit())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Reproducibility:}

\code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} pin the RNG seed and kind, so a run is
repeatable from its recorded \code{seed} for every model except \code{cubist}.
The Cubist C implementation is not bit-reproducible when
\code{committees > 1}: repeated fits on identical data under an identical
\code{set.seed()}, or under an explicit \code{cubistControl(seed = )}, can differ in
the fourth significant figure of a fold metric (measured on Cubist
0.6.0). \code{committees = 1} is stable. The effect is small, but it can flip
\code{select_best()} between near-tied grid points and move a reported test
metric by a few percent, so a \code{cubist} result should be read as
reproducible to that tolerance rather than exactly. \code{rf} and the other
engines are deterministic given \code{seed}. See GitHub issue #51.

\strong{Choosing models for large spectral libraries:}

Cost scales very differently with predictor count (\code{p}) across
\code{MODEL_SPECS}. \code{rf} (ranger), \code{xgboost}, \code{lightgbm} and \code{elastic_net}
(glmnet) stay fast at full spectral resolution; \code{plsr} is built for
\verb{p >> n} and is not a concern either. \code{cubist} fits a linear model in
every rule, so its cost grows sharply with \code{p}: on the KSSL clay library
at 4 cm-1 (14,228 rows x 851 predictors, ~12.1M cells), not one of 25
tune tasks finished in 29.5 minutes, while the same config with
\code{feature_selection = "pca"} finished in 368 s (test RPD 3.82) — OSSL's
published pipeline is SNV -> PCA(120) -> Cubist (Safanelli et al. 2025,
PLOS ONE 20(1):e0296545). \code{validate()}'s P010 check warns when a
\code{cubist} config with \code{feature_selection = "none"} sits on a table whose
\code{n_rows * n_predictors} exceeds \code{CUBIST_MAX_CELLS} (a conservative floor
well under the measured failure point, not a benchmark); the fix is
\code{feature_selection = "pca"}. \code{svm_rbf} (kernlab) and \code{mars} (earth) also
scale with \code{p} on well-established grounds — an RBF kernel matrix costs
\verb{O(n^2 p)} to build, and MARS's forward pass searches every predictor at
every candidate knot — but neither has been measured at library scale,
and \code{validate()} does not warn for them. A slow run with either on a
full-resolution table is a signal to add \code{feature_selection} there too.
See GitHub issue #40.

\strong{Recipe settings:}

\code{sg_window} and \code{pca_threshold} are set once per object, not per
configuration: every configuration's recipe uses the same value, and
neither is part of the config id. \code{sg_window} is the Savitzky-Golay window
in grid points. It trims \code{(sg_window - 1) / 2} columns from each end of the
spectrum for every preprocessing method, \code{"raw"} and \code{"snv"} included, so
it matters even where no filter runs. The polynomial order is not a
setting: each method fixes its own (\code{"sg"} order 1; \code{"deriv1"} first
derivative, order 1; \code{"deriv2"} second derivative, order 3; the \code{"snv_"}
variants the same), and the cubic is why the window must be at least 5.
At order 1 the \code{"sg"} smoother is a moving average, so widening
\code{sg_window} widens a boxcar: it flattens peaks and shoulders narrower than
the window rather than preserving them, as a higher-order Savitzky-Golay
filter would.

Because the window counts grid points, its physical width depends on the
axis it runs on, and \code{standardize()} can still change that axis after
\code{configure()}. So the width is not stored here: \code{configure()} prints it in
cm-1 for the axis it sees, \code{evaluate()} records the width it actually ran
in \code{evaluation$recipe}, and \code{evaluate()} refuses a window as wide as the
spectrum. \code{pca_threshold} is the share of variance kept by
\code{feature_selection = "pca"} and is read by nothing else. Both defaults are
the values the recipe ran before they were settable. See GitHub issue #62.

\strong{Outcome range:}

\code{outcome_range} is the physical range of the outcome, \code{c(lower, upper)},
either end possibly infinite. Every prediction the package scores or
serves is clamped to it: the test-set and out-of-fold predictions
\code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} score, the calibration predictions conformal
intervals are built from, \code{predict()}'s point predictions and interval
bounds, and the ensemble's. The default, \code{c(0, Inf)}, is the non-negative
floor the package applied before the range was settable, right for
concentrations, contents and most other soil properties. A signed property
such as δ13C or δ15N takes \code{c(-Inf, Inf)}, which clamps nothing, and a
bounded one such as a percentage may take \code{c(0, 100)}.

The range comes from what the property can physically be, not from the
data in hand. Setting it to the observed minimum and maximum clamps
predictions to the training range and, once test rows are in the table,
tunes the clamp on them. Prefer \code{-Inf} to an arbitrarily low finite floor:
the range's lower bound anchors the response bound \code{fit()} stores (see
\code{\link[=fit]{fit()}}), which allows half the span from that anchor above the training
maximum, so a distant floor loosens the guardrail. For outcomes between 10
and 20 the bound is 530 under \code{c(-1000, Inf)} and 25 under \code{c(-Inf, Inf)}.

The range is checked against the data: when an observed value of the
outcome lies outside it or is infinite, \code{configure()} aborts with class
\code{horizons_input_error}, and \code{evaluate()}, \code{fit()} and \code{ensemble()} repeat
the check before they fit anything. A range whose lower bound is negative
cannot be combined with the \code{"log"}, \code{"log10"} or \code{"sqrt"} transformations,
which are undefined for negative values and whose back-transforms floor
the predictions, and \code{evaluate()} and \code{fit()} refuse to rank by \code{"rrmse"}
under it, since its denominator, the mean outcome, can be zero or negative.
One range applies to the one outcome an object models; to model several
outcomes with different ranges, give each \code{configure()} call its own. See
GitHub issue #76.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# Minimal (single response auto-selected)
hd |> configure()

# Explicit outcome with multiple responses
hd |> configure(outcome = "SOC")

# Full experimental design
hd |> configure(
  outcome         = "SOC",
  models          = c("rf", "cubist", "plsr", "xgboost"),
  transformations = c("none", "log"),
  preprocessing   = c("raw", "snv", "sg"),
  cv_folds        = 10L,
  grid_size       = 20L
)

# A wider Savitzky-Golay window and a tighter PCA for every config
hd |> configure(
  preprocessing     = c("deriv1", "snv_deriv2"),
  feature_selection = "pca",
  sg_window         = 15L,
  pca_threshold     = 0.99
)

# A signed property: no floor at zero
hd |> configure(outcome = "d13C", outcome_range = c(-Inf, Inf))

# Multi-outcome pattern
c("SOC", "POM_C", "pH") |>
  purrr::map(~base |> configure(outcome = .x) |> evaluate() |> fit())
}

}
